WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:05.101
Nothing is absolute, certainly not in Hollywood,
and while it may be true that remakes are

00:00:05.101 --> 00:00:08.600
almost always worse than the originals, there
are some notable exceptions.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:12.440
In fact, some remakes aren't just as good
as the original, they're even better.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.320
Frank Sinatra, Dean Martin, Sammy Davis Jr.,
and the rest of the Rat Pack were riding high

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.600
in the early 1960s, so they decided to make
a movie.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.560
The result is Ocean's 11, a film which feels
like a big-budget vanity project because,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:26.960
well, it is.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.200
Frank Sinatra stars as Danny Ocean, who assembles
his 82nd Airborne buddies to rob five Las

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.020
Vegas casinos on New Year's Day.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.280
In 2001, Steven Soderbergh and a crew of Hollywood's
coolest remade Ocean's Eleven into the film

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:40.320
the original wanted to be.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.650
George Clooney assumes command as Danny Ocean,
with Matt Damon, Brad Pitt, and Julia Roberts

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:46.750
joining the crew.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:51.140
The resulting film showcases a heist story
that moves as confidently as its star-studded

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:51.840
cast.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.800
"Today's lesson: how to draw out the bluff.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.960
That much money this early in the game, I'm
guessing he's holding nothing more than a

00:00:58.960 --> 00:00:59.960
pair of face cards."

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.140
Disney was in a dark place in 1977.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:09.490
The studio's Golden and Silver Ages of the
1930s through the late 1960s were over, and

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:14.160
Disney was clearly adrift on the animation
front as well as in its live-action offerings.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:14.920
Case in point?

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.060
1977's Pete's Dragon.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.780
While the story of an orphan boy named Pete
and his magical dragon named Elliot effectively

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.880
mixes live action and hand-drawn animation,
that's about all it does well.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.260
However, studio execs saw something in the
story and brought it back almost 40 years

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.720
later in 2016's Pete's Dragon.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.891
The remake fared a bit better critically,
thanks to incredible special effects and a

00:01:36.891 --> 00:01:39.870
stellar live-action cast rounded out by Robert
Redford.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:45.320
John Wayne made a career out of playing John
Wayne, and in so doing became one of America's

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.060
most iconic movie stars ever.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:52.360
But out of all of Wayne's movies, only one
won him an Oscar: 1969's True Grit.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:58.000
Based on Charles Portis' 1968 novel, True
Grit tells the story of a young girl named

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:02.940
Mattie Ross who hires the drunken, eyepatch-wearing
U.S. Marshal "Rooster" Cogburn to hunt down

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.220
the man that killed her father.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.200
While Wayne's megawatt movie star bravado
made up for it, his True Grit was missing

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:10.800
Portis' deft prose.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:15.440
The Coen brothers made up for that 50 years
later, returning to Portis' novel to create

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.260
a more faithful adaptation.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.940
Jeff Bridges donned the eyepatch this time,
breathing new life into the whiskey-breathed

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.900
Rooster and securing an Oscar nomination along
the way.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.800
Unlike Wayne, Bridges didn't win an Oscar
for True Grit.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.220
Still, the 1969 original was more of a star
vehicle resting on Wayne's broad shoulders,

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.770
while the 2010 was top-to-bottom the better
film.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:39.000
"They tell me you're a man with true grit."

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:42.760
"What do you want, girl?

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.360
Speak up, it's suppertime."

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.590
The Thing From Another World is one of the
best 1950s sci-fi films, and although it boasts

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:53.760
no shortage of fine qualities, one main reason
for its success is the director.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.720
Not the credited director, Charles Nyby, but
the real director, Howard Hawks, who "presented"

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.560
the film, but didn't take a directing credit.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.459
Credit or no credit, make no mistake, this
is a Howard Hawks movie, with crackling dialogue,

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.380
competent, confident heroes, and a hawkish
worldview.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.620
That said, while audiences in 1951 were probably
scared stiff, viewers today wouldn't lose

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.100
any sleep over this film.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.960
That's not the case with John Carpenter's
The Thing, which came out in 1982.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.770
The first of Carpenter's Apocalypse Trilogy,
The Thing's truly terrifying SFX and storytelling

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:29.599
supersedes the original in the most important
way: it's much, much scarier, thanks in no

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.080
small part to a delightfully ambiguous ending.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.100
"What do we do?"

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:43.520
"Why don't we just wait here for a little
while, see what happens?"

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:49.879
Al Pacino and Robert De Niro's first onscreen
faceoff in Michael Mann's Heat was a cinematic

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:50.560
milestone.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.220
The two co-starred in The Godfather Part II
without sharing any screen time, so by the

00:03:55.220 --> 00:04:00.460
time Heat arrived in 1995, film fans were
ecstatic to finally see them trade lines.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:04.510
Heat proved a bit of a tease in this respect,
but the film's quality extends beyond its

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:05.200
star power.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:09.600
While this was the first time Pacino and De
Niro met onscreen, it wasn't the first time

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.060
this story was adapted.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:15.600
After making waves with TV's Miami Vice, Mann
wanted to make another cop drama on the golden

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.100
coast.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:20.700
His script eventually became a 1989 TV movie
called L.A. Takedown.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:25.800
Working with a TV-sized budget and lacking
the star-studded cast of its successor, Takedown

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.600
has gone on to become a footnote in Mann's
career, especially compared to Heat.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.840
It is one of Stephen King's most popular novels,
so it was inevitable that it'd become a movie

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:35.660
at some point.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:40.820
Still, this gruesome story about a supernatural
killer spanning decades doesn't exactly scream

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:42.120
TV miniseries.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.000
That was exactly what happened in 1990, however.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.620
While the miniseries isn't exactly bad, with
Tim Curry clearly relishing his role as Pennywise,

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.180
this two-part adaptation just lacks the visceral,
all-consuming terror the story demands.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:59.819
King's It is an epic story that demands an
R rating and all the bells and whistles that

00:04:59.819 --> 00:05:02.199
come with big-budget blockbuster filmmaking.

00:05:02.199 --> 00:05:06.889
In 2017, that's exactly what fans got, in
the form of the first chapter of a two-part

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:10.120
film adaptation liked by critics and loved
by moviegoers.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:15.270
Released two years later, It: Chapter Two
lacked the freshness and urgency of its predecessor.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:20.650
Still, while the miniseries has its fans,
the 2017 and 2019 movies are the definitive

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:23.400
screen versions of King's classic so far.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.819
Released in 1991, La Totale! is a comedy about
a humdrum civil servant who is actually a

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:31.560
crack secret agent, though his family has
no idea.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.919
Does this sound like the plot of James Cameron's
True Lies, one of the biggest hits of 1994

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.740
and the first movie to have a $100 million
budget?

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:39.440
It should!

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:44.400
True Lies is actually a remake of La Totale!,
only now with Arnold Schwarzanegger in the

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.160
role of the nerd/spy and Jamie Lee Curtis
as his unaware wife.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:53.099
La Totale! is perfectly fine for what it is,
but Cameron had a very different vision for

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:53.720
the story.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:58.680
He took the already absurd plot of La Totale!
and dialed it up to 11, transforming this

00:05:58.689 --> 00:06:03.889
silly French flick into a mega-budgeted, action-packed
blockbuster with bridges getting blown up,

00:06:03.889 --> 00:06:08.620
horses racing up skyscrapers, and Arnie blasting
a bad guy from the rocket of a harrier jet.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:12.320
It's a good thing he did, since True Lies
is a much better movie for it.

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:16.440
Critics dug it, while audiences showed up
in droves to make the film a massive success

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.500
domestically and around the world.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:23.680
When you think "great comedic duo," Steve
Martin and Michael Caine probably don't spring

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:24.980
immediately to mind.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:29.729
Yet the chemistry between the Texas-born,
California-raised superstar standup comedian

00:06:29.729 --> 00:06:34.900
and the Cockney-bred, two-time Academy Award-winning
English thespian is absolutely undeniable

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:36.440
in Dirty Rotten Scoundrels.

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:41.039
However, there was another pairing of an American
and a Brit in the same story that didn't have

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:45.789
quite the same chemistry: Marlon Brando and
David Niven in Bedtime Story.

00:06:45.789 --> 00:06:50.729
Released in 1964, Bedtime Story is about a
small-change scammer played by Brando and

00:06:50.729 --> 00:06:55.119
a high-class, cultured con artist played by
Niven who compete for innocent women's cash

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:56.000
in the Mediterranean.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.399
It was a great setup, but the film failed
to impress and fell into movie oblivion.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:05.319
However, sensing the promise of the premise,
Martin, Caine and director Frank Oz remade

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:09.220
it 24 years later in 1988 as Dirty Rotten
Scoundrels.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.360
It worked out pretty well, as the film went
on to be well-liked by critics and regular

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.400
moviegoers alike; it even inspired a 2005
Broadway Musical!

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:21.770
Hoping that lightning would strike twice,
the story was remade again 31 years later

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:26.569
in 2019 with Anne Hathaway and Rebel Wilson
as The Hustle, but critics weren't nearly

00:07:26.569 --> 00:07:27.140
as kind.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:31.660
Of the three versions of this story, we're
convinced Martin and Caine made the best con

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:32.660
artists.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:38.760
Inspired by advancing technology, Cold War
paranoia, and fears of nuclear war, the 1950s

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:40.460
was a golden age for sci-fi.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.540
Still, one film that truly stood out in that
era was 1958's The Fly.

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:50.020
Starring genre staple Vincent Price in a supporting
role, The Fly is about a scientist experimenting

00:07:50.039 --> 00:07:54.220
with a teleportation device who accidentally
ends up splicing his own atoms with those

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:55.200
of a house fly.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:08:01.240
Flash forward 28 years to 1986, David Cronenberg's
The Fly takes the unsettling source material

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:03.360
into much darker, more disturbing directions.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:08.260
In the hands of Cronenberg, The Fly became
arguably the best body horror film of all

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:08.760
time.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.820
It's a horror film that stays with you not
only because it's scary, but because it's

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:13.320
sad.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:18.020
While The Fly is one of the best sci-fi horror
films of the 1950s, Cronenberg's The Fly is

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:19.640
one of the best films, period.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:21.460
"We'll be the ultimate family.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:27.360
A family of three, joined together in one
body.

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:30.180
More human than I am alone."

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:35.980
In 1960, screenwriter Charles B. Griffith
and famed low-budget B-movie auteur Roger

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:39.520
Corman created a killer plant named Audrey
in The Little Shop of Horrors.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:44.080
The film is considered a cult classic today,
but Audrey's roots in American pop culture

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:46.060
were eventually to grow even deeper.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:50.480
Before making Disney billions as the lyricist
and composer for The Little Mermaid and Beauty

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:54.960
&amp; The Beast, Howard Ashman and Alan Menken
produced a stage musical version of Corman's

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:56.880
cult classic in 1982.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:02.220
This musical inspired the 1986 film version
starring Rick Moranis as Audrey's keeper,

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:05.140
with Steve Martin stealing scenes as a demented
dentist.

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:10.030
The 1986 version underperformed at the box
office, but it's since earned a sizable cult

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:11.040
following of its own.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:15.620
Has there ever been an actor who is able to
chew scenery like Al Pacino?

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:16.120
Maybe.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:21.680
Fifty years before Al Pacino told audiences
to say hello to his lil' friend as Tony Montana,

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:25.440
Paul Muni racked up an equally impressive
body count as Tony Camonte.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:31.300
Howard Hawks' 1932 Scarface has a story that
should be familiar to fans of Brian De Palma's

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:36.640
1983 remake: A low-ranking homicidal hood
works his way up the gangster corporate ladder,

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.540
sadistically wiping out his foes while maintaining
a soft spot for his little sister.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:45.720
Camonte is Italian, not Cuban, and instead
of peddling cocaine it's booze, but otherwise

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.000
the stories are the same.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:51.640
Hawks' Scarface is a classic, so what makes
the 1983 remake "better?"

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:56.710
Its exploration of excess continues to inspire
pop culture to this day, particularly hip-hop

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:57.320
culture.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:01.700
While both films are strong, Pacino's take
continues to resonate like few gangster films

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:03.580
ever have, before or since.

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:07.860
"So say goodnight to the bad guy, go on."

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:13.340
When you think of The Wizard of Oz, you probably
think of Judy Garland's bright red sparkling

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:17.020
shoes or maybe the lustrous, glittering gold
of the yellow brick road.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:19.940
You probably think of color, Technicolor to
be specific.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:24.300
However, while The Wizard of Oz was one of
the first films to be shot in color, it wasn't

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.160
the first time L. Frank Baum's story hit the
silver screen.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.760
Fourteen years before the Oz we all know and
love was released, there was another version.

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:36.800
The original 1925 version interpreted the
story as a heavy-handed slapstick silent comedy.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:41.220
Unfortunately, it isn't even funny, which
is saying something, considering it featured

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:42.860
Oliver Hardy as the Tin Man.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:47.320
Thankfully, movie studios still saw something
in the story and remade it in 1939.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:52.940
Hollywood just can't seem to get enough of
James Fenimore Cooper's Last of the Mohicans,

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:55.840
written in 1826, it's been filmed 11 times.

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:01.880
One of the earliest adaptations was the 1920
silent film version, followed by a 1936 version

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:05.200
starring Randolph Scott as Hawkeye and Bruce
Cabot as Magua.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:10.300
Besides the unpleasant whitewashing of the
Native American antagonist, the 1936 version

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:15.820
is otherwise an acceptable, if not particularly
memorable, 1930s Hollywood adventure picture.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:20.200
Nearly six decades later, director Michael
Mann would leave the sunny, pastel-colored

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:25.590
world of Miami Vice and take on this 18th-century
story, casting Daniel Day-Lewis as Hawkeye

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.880
and Native American actors to play Native
American characters.

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:32.740
Funnily enough, Mann is said to have based
the script for his film less on the novel

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:35.020
and more on the 1936 version.

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:38.740
Philip Dunne, that adaptation's screenwriter,
even received a credit.

